nodecode-protocol-chaos is a rewrite of chaos-xxl's chaos-xxl/protocol-chaos:

  https://github.com/chaos-xxl/protocol-chaos
  commit cb3f13a51b3d81e224eeadde68e1f55d6b2770d2
  Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Xue Xinglong (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is integrations/hermes/protocol-chaos of that commit.
Its seams are carried over onto Nodecode's: the plugin's pre_llm_call, which
put an overview of the open matters before the model and the matters a
message named into the turn, is the :round point here, and its post_llm_call,
which recorded the user's words after the answer, is the :frame point and a
worker. The wire is the same MCP streamable HTTP endpoint with the same
bearer token and the same protocol version, and the same three tools do the
work: context_pack for the directory (paged, cached thirty seconds, with
sinceVersion), event_get_protocol for one matter by id, and event_note to
record words verbatim. The matter states, the header line over added
context, the 3,900-character overview, the inverse-document-frequency
matching of a message against matter titles (with its 0.75 weight for a
closed matter), the indented forest of parts, the shape of the matter
block, the 600-character recording clip and the recording instructions the
model is given are the original's. So is the rule that only ids go to the
server when matters are read, and that a scheduled job's and a subagent's
turns get no context and record nothing (here: the whole cell stays out of
a turn it did not see the user's message of). The original's organizer
skill, which folds recorded words into goals, limits, plans and progress
once a day, is carried as the procedure (help :protocol-chaos) states, for
the user's own scheduled run.

What was left out, and why: the per-message structure fold
(event_record_expression driven by a structured-output call, with its
VERSION_CONFLICT retry) is not run after each turn here -- Nodecode's side
call answers prose, and the organizer procedure in the help topic does the
fold in one pass; the bundled Hermes cron blueprint for the organizer, since
a Nodecode user schedules it with the cron cell; the agent.secret_scope
lookup, replaced by the section's token, token_env and the
PROTOCOL_CHAOS_TOKEN the original already read; and the four overview
placement modes (system prompt, first turn, off), of which two remain:
the turn's first round, and off.
